A||||5||JohnnyK||What bolt grade to hold lower a-arm to frame..||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||07-04-2000||06:49 PM||||As some of you might know, the lower (rear of the car) front a-arm support in teh frame snapped, and I had to weld another one in.. Now I realized that I just grabbed any old bolt (same size, obviously) from the hardware store.  The bolt I took out was either black or very corroded. Is this bolt going to twist and snap, or is it fine? (The bolt that holds the lower (rear end) front a-arm to inside the engine cradle)||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||07-04-2000||07:04 PM||||I'd use grade 8 bolts, usually you can tell from the number of marks on the bolt head.||||reg||1
Z||000002||webbee||07-04-2000||07:05 PM||||Hardware store bolts are soft. Take the original to a machine shop. They have hard stuff. The grade of hardness is on the head. I forget the codes. Do it soon.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||07-05-2000||09:42 AM||||Some hardware stores have the stronger grades, but you often have to ask for them. I buy all my hardware at a tractor supply store. I buy regular, grade 5, or grade 8 bolts and nuts in bulk (by weight)...it's much cheaper. Sometimes the higher grades are dyed a different color, but not always. Uusually, the more little slashes on top the head of a bolt there is, then the higher the grade (the stronger it is).||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||07-06-2000||02:14 AM||||Looked at the bolt today, and there were no marks or lines, but "8.8" was stamped on the head (and written on the bin where I got it).. 8.8 was also written on most of the other bolt bins.. I have never heard of this before, so is this the grade? If so, is this good enough for that? (It's just a silver colour, not black like the original)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phaeton||07-06-2000||02:54 AM||||An 8.8 is equivalent to about a grade 5.<BR>A 10.9 is two steps up and should be available in hardware stores.  I've seen them color coded for strength but don't know if that's universal.||||reg||12
